      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "“Ever” is a flexible adverb in English that adds emphasis, asks about experiences, or strengthens hypothetical or extreme statements. It’s commonly used in questions, conditionals, emphatic expressions, and idioms."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Examples:",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Have you ever been to Japan?"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "If you ever need help, let me know."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "This is the best cake I’ve ever eaten!"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "⚠️ For Brazilian speakers, it’s common to confuse “ever” with “sempre” or misuse it as a simple intensifier like “muito.”"
          }
        ]
      },
